1
00:00:00,719 --> 00:00:05,719
The Great Gatsby chapter 1.

2
00:00:06,359 --> 00:00:10,919
In my younger and more vulnerable years my father gave me some advice that I’ve

3
00:00:10,919 --> 00:00:14,039
been turning over in my mind ever since.

4
00:00:14,039 --> 00:00:18,839
“Whenever you feel like criticizing anyone,” he told me, “just remember that all

5
00:00:18,839 --> 00:00:22,859
the people in this world haven’t had the advantages that you’ve had.”

6
00:00:22,859 --> 00:00:28,199
He didn’t say anymore but we’ve always been unusually communicative in a reserved way,

7
00:00:28,199 --> 00:00:31,980
and I understood that he meant a great deal more than that.

8
00:00:31,980 --> 00:00:37,320
In consequence, I’m inclined to reserve all judgments a habit that has opened up

9
00:00:37,320 --> 00:00:42,420
many curious natures to me and also made me the victim of not a few veteran boers

10
00:00:42,420 --> 00:00:47,100
The abnormal mind is quick to detect and attach itself to this quality when it

11
00:00:47,100 --> 00:00:51,238
appears in a normal person, and so it came about that in college I was

12
00:00:51,238 --> 00:00:55,979
unjustly accused of being a politician, because I was privy to the secret griefs

13
00:00:55,979 --> 00:00:58,500
of wild, unknown men.

14
00:00:58,500 --> 00:01:03,140
Most of the confidences were unsought— —frequently I have feigned sleep,

15
00:01:03,140 --> 00:01:09,239
preoccupation, or a hostile levity when I realized by some unmistakable sign that

16
00:01:09,239 --> 00:01:15,359
an intimate revelation was quivering on the horizon; for the intimate revelations of young

17
00:01:15,359 --> 00:01:20,938
men, or at least the terms in which they expressed them, are usually plagiaristic

18
00:01:20,938 --> 00:01:25,079
and marred by obvious suppressions.

19
00:01:25,079 --> 00:01:29,219
Reserving judgments is a matter of infinite hope.

20
00:01:29,219 --> 00:01:33,719
I am still a little afraid of missing something if I forget that, as my father

21
00:01:33,719 --> 00:01:40,739
snobbishly suggested, and I snobbishly repeat, a sense of the fundamental decencies is

22
00:01:40,739 --> 00:01:43,799
parceled out unequally at birth.

23
00:01:43,799 --> 00:01:50,099
And, after boasting this way of my tolerance, I come to the admission that it has a limit.

24
00:01:50,099 --> 00:01:54,780
Conduct may be founded on the hard rock or the wet marshes, but after a certain

25
00:01:54,780 --> 00:01:57,118
point I don’t care what it’s founded on.

26
00:01:57,118 --> 00:02:00,899
When I came back from the East last autumn I felt that I wanted the world to

27
00:02:00,899 --> 00:02:05,819
be in uniform and have a sort of moral attention forever; I wanted no more

28
00:02:05,819 --> 00:02:10,739
riotous excursions with privileged glimpses into the human heart.

29
00:02:10,739 --> 00:02:18,419
Only Gatsby, the man who gives his name to this book, was exempt from my reaction— —Gatsby,

30
00:02:18,419 --> 00:02:23,280
who represented everything for which I have an unaffected scorn.

31
00:02:23,280 --> 00:02:27,959
If personality is an unbroken series of successful gestures,

32
00:02:27,959 --> 00:02:32,818
then there was something gorgeous about him, some heightened sensitivity to the

33
00:02:32,818 --> 00:02:37,800
promises of life, as if he were related to one of those intricate machines that

34
00:02:37,800 --> 00:02:41,639
register earthquakes 10 000 miles away.

35
00:02:41,639 --> 00:02:45,598
This responsiveness had nothing to do with that flabby impressionability which

36
00:02:45,598 --> 00:02:49,800
is dignified under the name of the “creative temperament”—

37
00:02:49,800 --> 00:02:55,259
”—it was an extraordinary gift for hope, a romantic readiness such as I have

38
00:02:55,259 --> 00:03:01,500
never found in any other person and which it is not likely I shall ever find again.

39
00:03:01,500 --> 00:03:07,680
No— —Gatsby turned out all right in the end; it is what prayed on Gatsby,

40
00:03:07,680 --> 00:03:12,360
what foul dust floated in the wake of his dreams that temporarily closed out

41
00:03:12,360 --> 00:03:18,479
my interest in the abortive sorrows and short-winded elations of men.

42
00:03:18,479 --> 00:03:22,500
My family have been prominent well to do people in this Middle Western city for

43
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:26,699
three generations. The caraways are something of a clan, and we have a

44
00:03:26,699 --> 00:03:30,300
tradition that we’re descended from the Dukes of buckloo but the actual founder

45
00:03:30,300 --> 00:03:34,919
of my line was my grandfather’s brother, who came here in 51 sent a substitute to

46
00:03:34,919 --> 00:03:40,378
the Civil War, and started the wholesale hardware business that my father carries on today.

47
00:03:40,378 --> 00:03:44,699
I never saw this great uncle but I’m supposed to look like him— —with special

48
00:03:44,699 --> 00:03:49,378
reference to the rather hard-boiled painting that hangs in father’s office.

49
00:03:49,378 --> 00:03:54,780
I graduated from New Haven in 1915, just a quarter of a century after my father,

50
00:03:54,780 --> 00:04:01,680
and a little later I participated in that delayed Teutonic migration known as the Great War.

51
00:04:01,680 --> 00:04:06,959
I enjoyed the counter raid so thoroughly that I came back restless. Instead of

52
00:04:06,959 --> 00:04:10,680
being the warm center of the world of the Middle West now seemed like the

53
00:04:10,680 --> 00:04:16,798
ragged edge of the universe— —so I decided to go East and learn the bond business.

54
00:04:16,798 --> 00:04:21,839
Everybody I knew was in the bond business, so I supposed it could support one more single man.

55
00:04:21,839 --> 00:04:25,319
All my aunts and uncles talked it over as if they were choosing a prep school

56
00:04:25,319 --> 00:04:34,079
for me, and finally said, “Why yes with very grave, hesitant faces.

57
00:04:34,079 --> 00:04:38,779
Father agreed to finance me for a year, and after various delays I came East,

58
00:04:38,779 --> 00:04:43,500
permanently, I thought, in the spring of 22.

59
00:04:43,500 --> 00:04:47,939
The practical thing was to find rooms in the city, but it was a warm season, and I

60
00:04:47,939 --> 00:04:52,439
had just left a country of wide lawns and friendly trees, so when a young man

61
00:04:52,439 --> 00:04:56,279
at the office suggested that we take a house together in a commuting town, it

62
00:04:56,279 --> 00:04:58,079
sounded like a great idea.

63
00:04:58,079 --> 00:05:02,699
He found the house, a weather beaten cardboard bungalow at 80 a month,

64
00:05:02,699 --> 00:05:07,259
but at the last minute the firm ordered him to Washington, and I went out to the

65
00:05:07,259 --> 00:05:09,120
country alone.

66
00:05:09,120 --> 00:05:13,680
I had a dog— —at least I had him for a few days until he ran away—

67
00:05:13,680 --> 00:05:17,759
—and an old Dodge and a Finnish woman, who made my bed and cooked breakfast and

68
00:05:17,759 --> 00:05:22,259
muttered Finnish wisdom to herself over the electric stove.

69
00:05:22,259 --> 00:05:26,879
It was lonely for a day or so until one morning some man, more recently arrived

70
00:05:26,879 --> 00:05:29,399
than I, stopped me on the road.

71
00:05:29,399 --> 00:05:33,959
“How do you get to West Egg village?” he asked helplessly.

72
00:05:33,959 --> 00:05:35,459
I told him.

73
00:05:35,459 --> 00:05:41,519
And as I walked on I was lonely no longer. I was a guide, a pathfinder, an

74
00:05:41,519 --> 00:05:43,259
original settler.

75
00:05:43,259 --> 00:05:47,759
He had casually conferred on me the freedom of the neighborhood

76
00:05:47,759 --> 00:05:51,720
And so with the sunshine and the great bursts of leaves growing on the trees,

77
00:05:51,720 --> 00:05:56,939
just as things grow in fast movies, I had that familiar conviction that life

78
00:05:56,939 --> 00:06:00,180
was beginning over again with the summer.

79
00:06:00,180 --> 00:06:04,918
There was so much to read, for one thing, and so much fine health to be pulled out

80
00:06:04,918 --> 00:06:06,959
of the young breath-giving air.

81
00:06:06,959 --> 00:06:11,100
I bought a dozen volumes on banking and credit and investment securities, and

82
00:06:11,100 --> 00:06:15,600
they stood on my shelf in red and gold like new money from the mint, promising

83
00:06:15,600 --> 00:06:21,120
to unfold the shining secrets that only Midas and Morgan and messiness knew. And

84
00:06:21,120 --> 00:06:24,538
I had the high intention of reading many other books besides.

85
00:06:24,538 --> 00:06:29,459
I was rather literary in college— —one year I wrote a series of very solemn

86
00:06:29,459 --> 00:06:34,319
and obvious editorials for the Yale News— —and now I was going to bring back all

87
00:06:34,319 --> 00:06:39,418
such things into my life and become again that most limited of all specialists,

88
00:06:39,418 --> 00:06:46,680
the “well-rounded man.” This isn’t just an epigram— —life is much more successfully looked at

89
00:06:46,680 --> 00:06:49,860
from a single window, after all.

90
00:06:49,860 --> 00:06:53,220
It was a matter of chance that I should have rented a house in one of the

91
00:06:53,220 --> 00:06:55,560
strangest communities in North America.

92
00:06:55,560 --> 00:06:59,819
It was on that slender riotous island which extends itself due east of New

93
00:06:59,819 --> 00:07:07,680
York— —and where there are, among other natural curiosities, two unusual formations of land

94
00:07:07,680 --> 00:07:12,779
20 miles from the city a pair of enormous eggs, identical in contour and

95
00:07:12,779 --> 00:07:17,759
separated only by a courtesy bay jot out into the most domesticated body of salt

96
00:07:17,759 --> 00:07:24,418
water in the Western hemisphere, the great wet barnyard of Long Island Sound

97
00:07:24,418 --> 00:07:28,860
they're not perfect ovals— —like the AG in the Columbus story they're both crushed

98
00:07:28,860 --> 00:07:33,060
flat at the contact end— —but their physical resemblance must be a source of

99
00:07:33,060 --> 00:07:36,418
perpetual confusion to the goals that fly overhead.

100
00:07:36,418 --> 00:07:41,339
To the wingless a more arresting phenomenon is their dissimilarity in

101
00:07:41,339 --> 00:07:44,819
every particular except shape and size.

102
00:07:44,819 --> 00:07:50,038
I lived at West Egg, the— —well, the less fashionable of the two, though this is a

103
00:07:50,038 --> 00:07:56,220
most superficial tag to express the bizarre and not a little sinister contrast between them.

104
00:07:56,220 --> 00:08:01,439
My house was at the very tip of the egg, only 50 yards from the Sound, and

105
00:08:01,439 --> 00:08:07,978
squeezed between two huge places that rented for 12 or 15 000 a season.

106
00:08:07,978 --> 00:08:13,139
The one on my right was a colossal affair by any standard— —it was a factual

107
00:08:13,139 --> 00:08:17,699
imitation of some Hotel Deville in Normandy, with a tower on one side,

108
00:08:17,699 --> 00:08:23,098
spanking new under a thin beard of raw ivy, and a marble swimming pool, and more

109
00:08:23,098 --> 00:08:25,860
than 40 acres of lawn and garden.

110
00:08:25,860 --> 00:08:27,899
It was Gatsby’s mansion.

111
00:08:27,899 --> 00:08:33,000
Or, rather, as I didn’t know Mr. Gatsby, it was a mansion inhabited by a gentleman

112
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:34,320
of that name.

113
00:08:34,320 --> 00:08:39,059
My own house was an eyesore, but it was a small eyesore, and it had been overlooked,

114
00:08:39,059 --> 00:08:43,860
so I had a view of the water, a partial view of my neighbor's lawn, and the

115
00:08:43,860 --> 00:08:49,440
consoling proximity of millionaires— —all for eighty dollars a month.

116
00:08:49,440 --> 00:08:53,820
Across the courtesy bay the white Palace is a fashionable East Egg glittered

117
00:08:53,820 --> 00:08:57,958
along the water, and the history of the summer really begins on the evening I

118
00:08:57,958 --> 00:09:01,259
drove over there to have dinner with the Tom Buchanans.

119
00:09:01,259 --> 00:09:05,820
Daisy was my second cousin once removed, and I’d known Tom in college. And just

120
00:09:05,820 --> 00:09:09,059
after the war I spent two days with them in Chicago.

121
00:09:09,059 --> 00:09:12,600
Her husband, among various physical accomplishments, had been one of the most

122
00:09:12,600 --> 00:09:17,220
powerful ends that ever played football at New Haven— —a national figure in a way,

123
00:09:17,220 --> 00:09:22,080
one of those men who reached such an acute limited excellence at 21 that

124
00:09:22,080 --> 00:09:29,159
everything afterwards savers of anti-climax His family were enormously wealthy— —even

125
00:09:29,159 --> 00:09:33,240
in college his freedom with money was a matter for approach but now he’d left

126
00:09:33,240 --> 00:09:37,740
Chicago and come East in a fashion that rather took your breath away: for

127
00:09:37,740 --> 00:09:42,720
instance, he’d brought down a string of polo ponies from Lake Forest. It was hard

128
00:09:42,720 --> 00:09:47,339
to realize that a man in my own generation was wealthy enough to do that.

129
00:09:47,339 --> 00:09:50,399
Why they came East I don’t know.

130
00:09:50,399 --> 00:09:54,419
They had spent a year in France for no particular reason, and then drifted here

131
00:09:54,419 --> 00:09:58,679
and there unrestfully wherever people played polo and were rich together.

132
00:09:58,679 --> 00:10:04,799
This was a permanent move, said Daisy over the telephone, but I didn’t believe it—

133
00:10:04,799 --> 00:10:09,259
—I had no sight into Daisy’s heart, but I felt that Tom would drift on forever

134
00:10:09,259 --> 00:10:15,899
seeking, a little wistfully, for the dramatic turbulence of some irrecoverable football game.

135
00:10:15,899 --> 00:10:21,059
And so it happened that on a warm windy evening I drove over to East Egg to see

136
00:10:21,059 --> 00:10:24,600
two old friends whom I scarcely knew at all.

137
00:10:24,600 --> 00:10:29,100
Their house was even more elaborate than I expected, a cheerful red and white

138
00:10:29,100 --> 00:10:32,519
Georgian Colonial mansion, overlooking the bay.

139
00:10:32,519 --> 00:10:37,139
The lawn started at the beach and ran toward the front door for a quarter of a

140
00:10:37,139 --> 00:10:42,240
mile, jumping over sundials and brick walks and burning gardens— —finally when

141
00:10:42,240 --> 00:10:46,200
it reached the house drifting up the side in bright vines as though from the

142
00:10:46,200 --> 00:10:48,179
momentum of its run.

143
00:10:48,179 --> 00:10:53,100
The front was broken by a line of front windows, glowing now with reflected gold

144
00:10:53,100 --> 00:10:59,159
and wide open to the warm windy afternoon, and Tom Buchanan in riding clothes was

145
00:10:59,159 --> 00:11:02,759
standing with his legs apart on the front porch.

146
00:11:02,759 --> 00:11:05,639
He had changed since his New Haven years.

147
00:11:05,639 --> 00:11:11,940
Now he was a sturdy straw-haired man of 30 with a rather hard mouth and a supercilious manner.

148
00:11:11,940 --> 00:11:17,220
Two shining arrogant eyes had established dominance over his face and

149
00:11:17,220 --> 00:11:21,240
gave him the appearance of always leaning aggressively forward.

150
00:11:21,240 --> 00:11:27,600
Not even the effeminate swank of his riding clothes could hide the enormous power of that body—

151
00:11:27,600 --> 00:11:32,339
—he seemed to fill those glistening boots until he strained the top lacing, and you

152
00:11:32,339 --> 00:11:35,879
could see a great pack of muscle shifting when his shoulder moved under

153
00:11:35,879 --> 00:11:42,659
his thin coat. It was a body capable of enormous leverage— —a cruel body. His

154
00:11:42,659 --> 00:11:48,240
speaking voice, a gruff husky tenor, added to the impression of fractiousness he conveyed.

155
00:11:48,240 --> 00:11:53,578
There is a touch of paternal contempt in it, even toward the people he liked— —and

156
00:11:53,578 --> 00:11:57,059
there were men at New Haven who hated his guts.

157
00:11:57,059 --> 00:12:01,200
“Now, don’t think my opinion on these matters is final,” he seemed to say, “just

158
00:12:01,200 --> 00:12:04,620
because I’m stronger and more of a man than you are.”

159
00:12:04,620 --> 00:12:08,578
We were in the same senior society, and while we were never intimate I always

160
00:12:08,578 --> 00:12:11,820
had the impression that he approved of me and wanted me to like him with some

161
00:12:11,820 --> 00:12:14,759
harsh, defiant wistfulness of his own.

162
00:12:14,759 --> 00:12:17,940
We talked for a few minutes on the sunny porch.

163
00:12:17,940 --> 00:12:23,519
“I’ve got a nice place here,” he said, his eyes flashing about restlessly. Turning

164
00:12:23,519 --> 00:12:27,720
me around by one arm, he moved abroad flat hand along the front vista,

165
00:12:27,720 --> 00:12:33,240
including its sweep a sunken Italian garden, a half acre of deep, pungent roses,

166
00:12:33,240 --> 00:12:37,440
and a snub-nosed motorboat that bumped the tide offshore.

167
00:12:37,440 --> 00:12:43,200
“It belonged to domain the oil man.” He turned me around again, politely and abruptly.

168
00:12:43,200 --> 00:12:49,200
“We’ll go inside.” We walked through a high hallway into a bright Rosy colored space

169
00:12:49,200 --> 00:12:53,700
fragily bound into the house by French windows at either end.

170
00:12:53,700 --> 00:12:58,259
The windows were a jar and gleaming white against the fresh grass outside

171
00:12:58,259 --> 00:13:01,559
that seemed to grow a little way into the house.

172
00:13:01,559 --> 00:13:05,639
A breeze blew through the room blue curtains in at one end and out the other

173
00:13:05,639 --> 00:13:10,379
like pale flags, twisting them up toward the frosted wedding cake of the ceiling,

174
00:13:10,379 --> 00:13:15,299
and then rippled over the wine colored rug, making a shadow on it as wind does

175
00:13:15,299 --> 00:13:16,620
on the sea.

176
00:13:16,620 --> 00:13:21,179
The only completely stationary object in the room was an enormous couch on which

177
00:13:21,179 --> 00:13:25,620
two young women were buoyed up as though upon an anchored balloon.

178
00:13:25,620 --> 00:13:29,578
They were both in white, and their dresses were rippling and fluttering as

179
00:13:29,578 --> 00:13:33,899
if they had just been flown back in after a short flight around the house.

180
00:13:33,899 --> 00:13:37,559
I must have stood for a few moments listening to the whip and snap of the

181
00:13:37,559 --> 00:13:43,259
curtains and the groan of a picture on the wall there was a boom as Tom Buchanan shut

182
00:13:43,259 --> 00:13:47,879
the rear windows and the caught wind died out about the room, and the curtains

183
00:13:47,879 --> 00:13:52,919
and the rugs and the two young women ballooned slowly to the floor.

184
00:13:52,919 --> 00:13:57,419
The younger of the two was a stranger to me. She was extended full length at her

185
00:13:57,419 --> 00:14:02,100
end of the Devon completely motionless, and with her chin a little raised as if

186
00:14:02,100 --> 00:14:05,639
she were balancing something on it which was quite likely to fall.

187
00:14:05,639 --> 00:14:09,659
If she saw me out of the corner of her eyes she gave no hint of it—

188
00:14:09,659 --> 00:14:15,419
—indeed, I was almost surprised into murmuring an apology for having disturbed her by coming in.

189
00:14:15,419 --> 00:14:21,000
The other girl, Daisy, made an attempt to rise— —she leaned slightly forward with a

190
00:14:21,000 --> 00:14:25,799
conscientious expression— —then she laughed and absurd, charming little laugh,

191
00:14:25,799 --> 00:14:29,700
and I laughed too and I came forward into the room.

192
00:14:29,700 --> 00:14:35,399
“I’m paralyzed with happiness.” She laughed again, as if she said something very

193
00:14:35,399 --> 00:14:40,620
witty, and held my hand for a moment, looking up into my face, promising that

194
00:14:40,620 --> 00:14:45,240
there was no one in the world she so much wanted to see.

195
00:14:45,240 --> 00:14:47,039
That was a way she had.

196
00:14:47,039 --> 00:14:51,659
She hinted in a murmur that the surname of the balancing girl was Baker.

197
00:14:51,659 --> 00:14:55,500
(I’ve heard it said that Daisy’s murmur was only to make people lean toward her;

198
00:14:55,500 --> 00:14:59,698
an irrelevant criticism that made it no less charming.)

199
00:14:59,698 --> 00:15:04,620
At any rate, Miss Baker’s lips fluttered, she nodded at me almost imperceptibly,

200
00:15:04,620 --> 00:15:09,360
and then quickly tipped her head back again— —the object she was balancing had

201
00:15:09,360 --> 00:15:13,198
obviously taught it a little and given her something of a fright.

202
00:15:13,198 --> 00:15:16,919
Again a sort of apology arose to my lips.

203
00:15:16,919 --> 00:15:23,940
Almost any exhibition of complete self-sufficiency draws a stunned tribute from me.

204
00:15:23,940 --> 00:15:29,278
I looked back at my cousin, who began to ask me questions in her low, thrilling voice.

205
00:15:29,278 --> 00:15:34,559
It was the kind of voice that the ear follows up and down, as if each speech is

206
00:15:34,559 --> 00:15:38,519
an arrangement of notes that will never be played again.

207
00:15:38,519 --> 00:15:43,980
Her face was sad and lovely with bright things in it, bright eyes and a bright

208
00:15:43,980 --> 00:15:48,000
passionate mouth, but there was an excitement in her voice that men who had

209
00:15:48,000 --> 00:15:54,299
cared for her found difficult to forget: a singing compulsion, a whispered

210
00:15:54,299 --> 00:15:59,820
“Listen,” a promise that she had done gay, exciting things just a while since and

211
00:15:59,820 --> 00:16:04,500
that they were gay, exciting things hovering in the next hour.

212
00:16:04,500 --> 00:16:06,539
I told her how I had stopped off in

213
00:16:06,539 --> 00:16:10,500
Chicago for a day on my way East, and how a dozen people had sent their love

214
00:16:10,500 --> 00:16:11,639
through me.

215
00:16:11,639 --> 00:16:14,339
“Do they miss me?” she cried ecstatically.

216
00:16:14,339 --> 00:16:18,839
“The whole town is desolate. All the cars have the left rear wheel painted black

217
00:16:18,839 --> 00:16:22,679
