Nordic race. Yes, I’m sure we did. It sort

426
00:32:34,980 --> 00:32:38,519
of crept up on us and first thing you know—” “Don’t believe everything you hear,

427
00:32:38,519 --> 00:32:43,980
Nick,” he advised me. I said lightly that I had heard nothing at all, and a few

428
00:32:43,980 --> 00:32:46,859
minutes later I got up to go home.

429
00:32:46,859 --> 00:32:52,200
They came to the door with me and stood side by side in a cheerful square of light.

430
00:32:52,200 --> 00:32:57,538
As I started my motor Daisy peremptorally called: “Wait!

431
00:32:57,538 --> 00:33:00,839
“I forgot to ask you something, and it’s important.

432
00:33:00,839 --> 00:33:06,298
We heard you were engaged to a girl out West.” “That’s right,” corroborated Tom

433
00:33:06,298 --> 00:33:12,558
kindly. “We heard that you were engaged.” “It’s liable I’m too poor.” “But we heard it,”

434
00:33:12,558 --> 00:33:17,819
insisted Daisy, surprising me by opening up again in a flower-like way. “We heard

435
00:33:17,819 --> 00:33:22,558
it from three people, so it must be true.” Of course I knew what they were

436
00:33:22,558 --> 00:33:27,298
referring to, but I wasn’t even vaguely engaged. The fact that gossip had

437
00:33:27,298 --> 00:33:31,200
published the bands was one of the reasons I'd come East. You can’t stop

438
00:33:31,200 --> 00:33:35,700
going with an old friend on account of rumors and on the other hand I had no

439
00:33:35,700 --> 00:33:39,119
intention of being rumored into marriage.

440
00:33:39,119 --> 00:33:44,220
Their interest rather touched me and made them less remotely rich—

441
00:33:44,220 --> 00:33:48,778
—nevertheless, I was confused and a little disgusted as I drove away.

442
00:33:48,778 --> 00:33:51,179
It seemed to me that the thing for Daisy

443
00:33:51,179 --> 00:33:55,500
to do was to rush out of the house, child in arms— —but apparently there was no such

444
00:33:55,500 --> 00:33:57,119
intention in her head.

445
00:33:57,119 --> 00:34:01,798
As for Tom, the fact that he “had some woman in New York” was really less

446
00:34:01,798 --> 00:34:05,759
surprising than that he had been depressed by a book.

447
00:34:05,759 --> 00:34:11,099
Something was making him nibble at the edge of stale ideas as if his sturdy

448
00:34:11,099 --> 00:34:16,079
physical egotism no longer nourished his peremptory heart.

449
00:34:16,079 --> 00:34:20,878
Already it was deep summer on Road House roofs and in front of wayside garages,

450
00:34:20,878 --> 00:34:25,500
where new red gas pump sat out in pools of light,

451
00:34:25,500 --> 00:34:27,659
and when I reached my estate at West Egg

452
00:34:27,659 --> 00:34:32,699
I ran the car under its shed and sat for a while on an abandoned grass roller in

453
00:34:32,699 --> 00:34:34,079
the yard.

454
00:34:34,079 --> 00:34:38,519
The wind had blown off, leaving a loud, bright night, with wings beating in the

455
00:34:38,519 --> 00:34:42,838
trees and a persistent organ sound as the full bellows of the earth blew the

456
00:34:42,838 --> 00:34:45,300
frogs full of life.

457
00:34:45,300 --> 00:34:49,619
The silhouette of a moving cat wavered across the moonlight, and, turning my head

458
00:34:49,619 --> 00:34:55,378
to watch it, I saw that I was not alone 50 feet away a figure had emerged from

459
00:34:55,378 --> 00:34:59,519
the shadow of my neighbor's mansion and was standing with his hands in his

460
00:34:59,519 --> 00:35:03,539
pockets regarding the silver pepper of the stars.

461
00:35:03,539 --> 00:35:08,039
Something in his leisurely movements and the secure position of his feet upon the

462
00:35:08,039 --> 00:35:13,199
lawn suggested that it was Mr. Gatsby himself, come out to determine what Cher

463
00:35:13,199 --> 00:35:16,199
was his of our local heavens.

464
00:35:16,199 --> 00:35:20,400
I decided to call to him. Miss Baker had mentioned him at dinner, and that would

465
00:35:20,400 --> 00:35:22,559
do for an introduction.

466
00:35:22,559 --> 00:35:27,000
But I didn’t call to him, for he gave a sudden intimation that he

467
00:35:27,000 --> 00:35:33,420
was content to be alone— —he stretched out his arms toward the dark water in a curious way

468
00:35:33,420 --> 00:35:38,219
as far as I was from him, I could have sworn he was trembling.

469
00:35:38,219 --> 00:35:42,599
Involuntarily I glanced seaward— —and distinguished nothing except a single

470
00:35:42,599 --> 00:35:48,960
green light, minute and far away, that might have been the end of a dock.

471
00:35:48,960 --> 00:35:56,039
When I looked once more for Gatsby he had vanished, and I was alone again

472
00:35:56,039 --> 00:35:59,000
